as  personally responsible for allowing  jimmy savile to escape justice there was  no fair or reasonable basis for that  assertion this was not the usual cut and  thrust of politics it was an  inappropriate and partisan reference to  a horrendous case of child sex abuse you  tried to clarify your position today but  despite my urging you did not apologize  for the misleading impression you gave  so we can tell just from that you know  quite a row about that she told him to  apologize properly and and and this one  did not this morning he gave a  clarification what she clearly wanted  was an explicit apology  and with that she has left down in the  street and that was a genuine shock  because she has been such a loyal ally  so that's uh  manira and her position we then tonight  had  the director of communications  doyle resigning and in the past few  minutes the two most senior figures in  number 10 dan rosenfeld the chief of  staff and martin reynolds the private  principal secretary that's effectively  the most senior official advising the  prime minister he is the man that sent  the bring your own booze email around to  staff now all three of these gentlemen  will no doubt be implicated in the sue  grey full report when it comes out  because they have been publicly linked  to some of the events  that she is investigating so there is  clearly a question dermot were all three  of these heads go into role  when the ful when the truth outs  around the party there have been  expectations here saying all three of  them will have to step down from their  jobs  but the manner in which this is  happening is uncontrolled  you would expect the prime minister said  on monday i'm going to have to shake up  my top team i will come and bring you  the news what you have instead is a  series of resignations into the evening  on a thursday and and  it doesn't look as if the prime minister  is in control of it although  he has he instantly replaced uh didn't  he mercer he's got it he's got a new  policy chief in andrew griffith an mp an  mp and actually who formerly was a very  senior manager at sky  he has he's he's his his sort of  parliamentary aide he's made andrew his  policy adviser the statement which i've  you forgive me i i will pull up on my  phone here this is the statement from  downing street about dan rosenfeld and  martin reynolds both offering their  resignations the prime and accepting  this thanking them for their significant  contribution to number 10.  it says they will continue in their  roles while successes are appointed uh  and recruitment for both posts is  underway now it might be  that the prime minister tries to present  this and his sort of inner circle  as the beginnings of that shake-up and  that that clear out of downing street  but the manner in which it is happening  suggests the prime minister that is not  in control  uh resignations coming out of the blue  very  destabilizing and i think there's also  another question all of this dermot  if you are looking  uh into number 10 if you are a business  leader or you have a successful career  in other parts of government  are you going to want to go into number  10 and work with this current  administration given uh what is  happening there currently and the chaos  that this operation  is in and as someone that is outside  looking in but deals with these people  uh in my job  i'm looking at going well who do i go to  who do i ask questions of it really  feels like  the the ship is being deserted at the  moment and the prime minister has to try  and get a grip but it feels very very  very unstable and these are officials  but what about the politicians we  already know of at least 13 letters  having gone into the 1922 committee  asking for a vote of confidence in the  prime minister and and just before all  this started we were hearing from the  chancellor rishi sonak about the the  support for the cost of living crisis  when he was asked about those comments  that boris johnson made about  kierstarman and jimmy savile and he said  uh well let's let's listen to the grab i  was going to quote him but uh we've  actually got that from the conference  and and give us your assessment what  effect this has had  first thing to say is you know she was a  valued colleague i very much enjoyed  working with her and i'm sorry to see  her leave government on miss working  with her  and with regard to their comments  in you know being being honest i  wouldn't have said it and i'm glad that  the prime minister clarified what he  meant  being honest i wouldn't have said it now  other members of the cabinet who who  have appeared have been a bit more  supportive yeah i just literally wrote  down on my pit of paper cut and thrust  which is how dominic robb defended it he  said this was all part of the cut and  thrust of the chamber the remarks over  jimmy savile trying to defend the prime  minister what you saw there  from the chancellor  is a politician who's not trying to  defend the prime minister he's actually  trying to put distance between himself  and the prime minister he wasn't being  overtly disloyal  by actually verbally saying i don't  support him but the fact that he in  politics sometimes what you don't say  tells us all quite a lot and the fact  that he didn't give him his sort of  unqualified support and tried to defend  him tells us everything we need to know  about the way rishi sanak is positioning  trying to distance being slightly  critical actually there in fact not  slightly i took it as him being critical  and it also shows that boris johnson's  moral authority  uh with his mps and his cabinet is  draining away because russia sort of  thinks well i can and that begs the next  question which i'm going to put to you  which i already know you can't answer  because you're sitting here talking to  me which is how is it going down with  the with the with the politicians  particularly those that sit around boris  johnson at the cabinet table their  support or lack of it crucial right now  well i can tell you guys i did speak to  one ally after the jack doyle the  director of communications resignation  and manira merza's resignation  and that figure said to me  that in the case of jack doyle there's  complexities around the parties and  you know as i was saying earlier  all three of those gentlemen had other  difficulties but in the case of  manira mazza  this figure said to me well i agree with  her i think he should have apologized  and that's very telling that actually  the prime minister has had to perform a  series of u-turns a series of missteps  this week over things he said and then  he's had to change his mind  and his party are looking on saying  you're going to be getting a grip and  you are not getting a grip and you are  not changing your behavior so it begs  the question  do mps think  it's time for him to go and let us go in

